}
#TopStatusAudioBtn:pressed { color: #ffffff; border-color: #f5a623; }

#TopStatusAudioBtn { margin-left: 6px; padding: 0 2px; min-width: 40px; }

/* ---- the three stems, one stack per waveform lane ---- */

/* rekordbox's own words and its own colours, read out of rekordbox.exe rather
   than matched by eye. The four juce::Colour values its STEMS strip is built
   from are one array at 0x145D3B950, filled by the static initialiser at file
   offset 0x0056CA00, and the sibling name array at 0x145D3B8D0 names them
   INST, VOCAL, DRUMS, BASS in that order. BASS is the fourth and has no cell
   here; the three that do are DRUMS #4655ff, VOCAL #02da0c and INST #da1b02.

   Centred, which is also the only alignment worth having here: WPushButton
   paints its own text into its own rect, so a stylesheet text-align never
   reaches it and the XML's left alignment took the vertical centring with it.
   `color` does reach it -- that one comes off the palette. Black there is the
   reference picture's choice and not the binary's: rekordbox puts the stem
   colour into the text and outline slots over a background it resolves at
   runtime, so the fills above are the only part of this taken from it. */
#StemStack {
  margin: 1px 4px 1px 0;
}

#StemDrums, #StemVocal, #StemInst {
  border: 1px solid #0a0f18;
  border-radius: 2px;
  color: #000000;
  font-size: 12px;
  font-weight: bold;
  margin: 1px 0;
}

#StemDrums { background-color: #4655ff; }
#StemVocal { background-color: #02da0c; }
#StemInst { background-color: #da1b02; }

/* Engaged: each colour taken two fifths of the way to white, with a white edge,
   so the state carries from as far away as the waveform does and the black
   lettering only gains contrast by it. */
#StemDrums[value="1"] { background-color: #9099ff; border-color: #ffffff; }
#StemVocal[value="1"] { background-color: #67e96d; border-color: #ffffff; }
#StemInst[value="1"] { background-color: #e97667; border-color: #ffffff; }


/* ---- the four stems Mixxx 2.6 gives us, one strip per waveform lane ---- */

/* This block styles widgets that only ever appear on 2.6 with a stem track
   loaded. On the box as it stands the strip is built and never shown, so
   nothing here reaches the screen; it is written now so that the day the box
   moves to 2.6 the strip is not unstyled.

   The stem's own colour is not on screen, and it took a measurement to be
   sure it could not be. WStemLabel writes the manifest's colour into the
   widget's palette, but a stylesheet `color` beats a palette, and the
   `WLabel { color: #e5e6ea; }` rule far above reaches WStemLabel because a Qt
   type selector matches subclasses too. `color: palette(window-text)` looks
   like the way out and is not: the stylesheet resolves that once, while the
   label is still wearing the default palette, and re-applies the black it
   resolved every time the label is repolished. Measured on 2.6 with the
   4-stem test file, whose manifest asks for #fd4a4a, #ffff00, #00e8e8 and
   #ad65ff, all four names came out #010203. The only fix is to narrow the
   global rule to `.WLabel`, which would take the colour off every Number,
   Key, Time and track title in the skin as well, so the names are grey and
   the words carry the meaning. */
#StemStrip {
  margin: 1px 4px 1px 0;
}

#StemRow {
  border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a22;
  qproperty-layoutAlignment: 'AlignVCenter';
}

#StemName {
  color: #e5e6ea;
  font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: bold;
  padding-left: 4px;
}

#StemMute {
  border: 1px solid #32323c;
  border-radius: 2px;
  color: #6f7079;
  font-size: 9px;
  font-weight: bold;
  margin: 0 4px;
}

/* Muted is the lit state, because the word on the button is MUTE. Pioneer's
   own red for a cut channel, and the lettering goes to white with it. */
#StemMute[value="1"] {
  background-color: #d73535;
  border-color: #ffffff;
  color: #ffffff;
}

#StemVolume, #StemColor {
  background-color: transparent;
  margin-right: 4px;
}

